 \page page_conventions_home Coding Conventions and IDEs

\tableofcontents

 A header file declares the classes and the functions of its namespace. The matching cpp file
 defines them. The matching file with the extension .cpph defines the inline functions and the
 templates. The header includes this file at its end.

 The Doxygen block of a function stands at its definition. The declaration in the header stays
 bare. A function whose body needs comments to explain it needs a simpler structure or a split
 into smaller functions.

 clang-format formats every C++ file with the style file .clang-format of the repository root. A
 line ends at 120 characters.


 Syntax Conventions
 ==================

 - One file per class
 - One subdirectory per namespace
 - Descriptive names, with acronyms in uppercase (getCFLFactor())


# Python Conventions / Style Guide

Most users interact with Peano through the Python API. It assembles the C++ snippets of an
application. Each experiment can modify them.

Our Python code follows the [PEP-8 style guide for Python](https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/).
A method name uses lowercase words joined by underscores:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
def my_method_for_calculating_residuals(*args):
  # code goes here
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

A class name uses CamelCase:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
class MyClassForStoringData:
  # code goes here
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

More specifically:

- A module has a short, all-lowercase name. An underscore in a module name separates words where it
  improves readability. A package has a short, all-lowercase name without underscores.
- A class name uses the CapWords (CamelCase) convention.
- A function name uses lowercase words joined by underscores.
- A variable name follows the convention of function names.

# C++ conventions / Style Guide

- Types and the values of an enum class use PascalCase. Functions, methods and local variables use
  camelCase. Private and protected members start with an underscore.
- Indentation uses two spaces and no tabs.
- Every body of an if, an else and a loop stands in braces. The opening brace stays on the line of
  the statement:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  // code goes here
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The formatting and analysis scripts described below apply these conventions to every
source file of the repository.


Code Formatting and Static Analysis
===================================

The package ``postprocessing`` contains one script per tool. Each script walks every source
file below the repository root and skips build directories, generated code and virtual
environments. Run them from the repository root, after ``pip install -r requirements.txt``
installed the tools:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 -m postprocessing.apply_clang_format
python3 -m postprocessing.apply_black
python3 -m postprocessing.apply_clang_tidy --build-directory build
python3 -m postprocessing.check_includes
python3 -m postprocessing.check_imports
python3 -m postprocessing.check_python_lint
python3 -m postprocessing.check_dead_code
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

- ``apply_clang_format`` formats the C and C++ files with ``.clang-format``.
- ``apply_black`` formats the Python files.
- ``apply_clang_tidy`` runs ``.clang-tidy`` on the translation units of the compilation
  database of a CMake build and on the headers they include; ``--fix`` applies the fixes.
- ``check_includes`` checks the include conventions of the C and C++ files and, with a
  compilation database, reports unused and indirect includes.
- ``check_imports`` checks the order of the Python imports, reports unused imports and
  imports every module of a package in a fresh interpreter.
- ``check_python_lint`` reports syntax errors, undefined names and unused variables.
- ``check_dead_code`` reports unused functions, classes and variables.

With ``--check``, the formatting scripts list the files they would change and change
none. A script exits with status one when it reports a finding.



